  "query_preview": "..Tonight, a trailing s/wv will move around the \nsouthwestern side of the parent low that brought moisture to NW \nArizona last night. This wave is deeper and colder, but contains \nalmost no moisture above 600-700mb. However, it will drive another \nwell-defined cold front through our area, as well as a brief period \nof dynamical forcing. Additionally, decent upslope flow continue and \nboundary layer moisture remains robust through central Arizona.\n\nLooking at model soundings during FROPA tonight, we see that the \nFlagstaff area could saturate to around -8C and over eastern Arizona \nmuch later tonight, to around -14C. This is usually more than enough \nfor scattered showers along the I-17 corridor, with higher coverage \nover Apache County as the front becomes a bit more consolidated and \nsaturation improves. The air mass is cold enough for snow at least \ndown to 6000 feet, if not lower. The lack of dendrites, at first, in \nthe Flagstaff area, will make early composition mainly snow grains \nand pellets. Dendritic formation is then likely later tonight over \nApache County. This will result in patchy snow amounts from a few \ntenths to around an inch, but a band of 1-2 inches may form later \nover southern Apache County.\n\nThis is not a widespread or particularly impactful event, but be \naware that some slick spots may form in the High Country tonight.\n\nBehind the front, expect cooler and dry conditions for the weekend. \nVery cold overnight readings are possible over higher terrain. The \nremainder of the forecast then becomes dominated by the increasing \nlikelihood of a strong SE Pacific ridge, sending most energy riding \nup and over to our north. Very slight chances persist due to these \noccasional waves Monday and Wednesday, but confidence is low and any \nlow QPF will likely be along the Utah border.",
